lester schum is a district councillor  now facing the prospect of life in  prison after being charged  with violating hong kong's tough new  national security law  if he's convicted his political career  like his freedom will be over  on sunday he was one of 47 activists  told to appear at police stations  across the territory with his wife of  four weeks by his side  shum insisted the fight for democracy in  hong kong  must go on i want to tell all of  the hong kong people no matter where you  are to keep  faithful and to be hopeful and to  continue our struggle  lester do you believe you'll be  disqualified no i'm not coming  i had asked shum that same question  seven months ago  after he was detained for taking part in  a small protest  in breach of social distancing rules i  really don't know  whether i would be disqualified or not  but i can say for sure that  there is a chance for being happy shum  was a candidate in an  unofficial poll held last july to  pre-select candidates  for legislative elections due two months  later  elections which were later cancelled  with the government citing covert 19.  the strategy of the pro-democracy camp  had been this  gain a simple majority in parliament  block important legislation  and forced the resignation of the  territories leader carrie lamb  but such tactics common in democracies  around the world  are enough to get you arrested in  today's hong kong  and in january the police came knocking  rounding up 55  activists including u.s human rights  lawyer john clancy  who was not charged on sunday but joshua  wong  the territory's best known pro-democracy  voice was  he's already in jail after being  convicted of organizing  an unauthorized protest two years ago  when the beijing-imposed national  security law took effect here  kari lam said it would only affect a  small number of people  so far it's led to almost a hundred  arrests  with half that number now charged human  rights groups say that hong kong is  heading in an authoritarian direction  with new laws and regulations curbing  freedoms  once promised by china gradually the  voices of descent  are being silenced adrian brown al  jazeera  hong kong
 

